We thank the reviewer for the thoughtful comments and questions. We address each of them below:

> It is unclear how/where serialisation/data marshalling is to be implemented

Serialization/data marshaling is implemented by the shared program state system developer, not the program developer. Specifically, the system developer must implement a serialization function $\alpha: v \rightarrow v_N$ and reification function $\gamma: v_N \rightarrow v$ that Nightjar calls on the values to be resumed and the parameters of the effects. Concretely, the formalism is as follows:
```
handle_N e_N with {
    finally v_N -> γ(v_N);
    Lookup(x) -> resume α(x);
    Assign(x, v_N) -> let x = γ(v_N) in resume α(());
    Deref(a) -> resume α(!γ(a));
    Ref(v_N) -> resume α(ref γ(v_N));
    Labels() -> resume α(labels);
    Goto(l, v_N) -> goto γ(l) γ(v_N);
}
```
where $v_N$ are NFI values and $v$ are host values. We have incorporated this clarification to the NFI in the revised draft.

> it is also unclear if grammar-based sampling is compatible with the approach or if any such grammar file should be augmented to enable emission of effect tokens.

Assuming the reviewer is asking whether grammar-based structured output by the LLM is compatible with generating effects during program execution, we make the following clarification: Our implementation uses JSON format to encode the effects in order to enforce structured output, but other LLM response formats are also viable design choices that the system developer may choose. We have supplied a straightfoward implementation in the paper to demonstrate the efficacy of enabling executable shared program state programs.

We kindly request the reviewer to clarify their question if we have not addressed it. 

> perhaps out of scope, but the handler loop seems unintuitive: if emitted effects are side-effect free wrt the program state that is observed by the NL code, why not stage an execution plan and delay the interrupts until the last moment possible to reduce overhead?

The handler presented in Figure 3 is a formal specification of the semantics of the interface. Nightjar's implementation with an LLM agent loop is one implementation of the formal specification. There is a tradeoff between an interpreter approach (i.e. Nightjar) and a just-in-time (JIT) compiled (i.e. staged execution plan) approach. In the interpreted approach, the LLM can adapt the next step to the result of the previous effects. Even side-effect free ones that only read values from the program state can change the trajectory of the following effects. The cost to this adaptibility is runtime overhead. In the JIT-compiled approach, the runtime overhead of LLM roundtrips would be reduced, but the LLM cannot adapt to intermediate program values. The goal of this work is to present the shared program state abstraction and demonstrate that it can be realized as executable programs. We leave deeper exploration of different execution and compilation strategies for future work.

> perhaps missing reference to literate programming (of ye olden days): Knuth, Donald Ervin. "Literate programming." The computer journal 27.2 (1984): 97-111.

We have incorporated Literate Programming to Related Work. 

> There is an unexplored trade-off frontier: in the paper, the comparison is the paradigm handling the boilerplate vs the user-written boilerplate. A fully FL/PL program vs a hybrid program would be another comparison of interest, but the cost normalisation is more difficult.
